{"name":"Ginseng Root","hex":"#e6cdb5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ginseng","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["ginseng; used as a specific epithet"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of two species of the genus Panax (Panax ginseng and Panax quinquefolius), having forked roots supposed to have medicinal and aphrodisiac properties.","The root of such a plant, or an extract of these roots."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ginseng"},{"term":"root","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The part of a plant, generally underground, that anchors and supports the plant body, absorbs and stores water and nutrients, and in some plants is able to perform vegetative reproduction.","A root vegetable."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grow roots; to enter the earth, as roots; to take root and begin to grow.","To prepare, oversee, or otherwise cause the rooting of cuttings."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/root"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ginseng","blocks":[{"text":"Ginseng is the root of plants in the genus Panax, such as South China ginseng (P. notoginseng), Korean ginseng (P. ginseng), and American ginseng (P. quinquefolius), characterized by the presence of ginsenosides and gintonin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Insam_%28ginseng%29.jpg/330px-Insam_%28ginseng%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginseng","wikidata":"Q20817212"},{"title":"Root","blocks":[{"text":"In vascular plants, the roots are the organs of a plant that are modified to provide anchorage for the plant and take in water and nutrients into the plant body, which allows plants to grow taller and faster. They are most often below the surface of the soil, but roots can also be aerial or aerating, that is, growing up above the ground or especially above water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo